{"about":"20b35eec8f966227f76f4aa3b7bd3ff43fbc8f61e67d869cfe6502d6db030ca0","body":"The claim that choosing the better-corroborated theory is 'logically indistinguishable from flipping a coin' ignores the asymmetry of risk in critical decisions. While Popper denies we can know which theory is true, he argues we can know which theory has been more rigorously tested and has survived. To ignore this track record is not neutrality; it is an irrational disregard for available empirical data regarding a theory's robustness. The preference is defensible as a pragmatic imperative to utilize the most stringently vetted information available, which constitutes a rational, non-inductive strategy for minimizing the risk of immediate error.\n[would settle: An agreed-upon definition of 'rationality' in decision theory that separates expected utility maximization (which often relies on induction) from risk-minimization based on stress-test history.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}